================================================================================
ABLATION: which half of the fix does what
Two mechanisms deliver the driver libraries under a name the loader will find:
  (A) mount the host file a SECOND time at its SONAME path  (host side)
  (B) run ldconfig inside the container from a guest CDI hook (guest side)
Both were run alone and together, on nvidia/cuda:13.3.1-cudnn-devel-ubuntu26.04.
================================================================================

--- (A) alone: SONAME mounts, no hook -------------------------------------
$ docker run --runtime=nvkvm-kata --gpus all --rm <img> nvidia-smi
  WARNING: The NVIDIA Driver was not detected.  GPU functionality will not
     be available.                       <-- the image's own check, see below
  NVIDIA-SMI 575.51.03   Driver Version: 575.51.03   CUDA Version: 12.9
  0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070 Ti SUPER ...
$ ... gcc -O2 -o /tmp/v /tmp/vecadd.c -ldl && /tmp/v
  elements: 1048576  mismatches: 0   VECADD OK

  So (A) alone WORKS for dlopen and for CUDA -- the SONAME path is a real file
  in a directory glibc searches by default, so no cache is needed.  But
  /etc/ld.so.cache stays empty of NVIDIA entries, and NVIDIA's own images gate
  on exactly that:

    /opt/nvidia/entrypoint.d/50-gpu-driver-check.sh
      _LIBCUDA_FROM_LD_CACHE=$(ldconfig -p | grep libcuda.so.1)
      ...
      if [[ -z "${_LIBCUDA_FOUND}" || -z "${_DRIVER_FOUND}" ]]; then
        echo "WARNING: The NVIDIA Driver was not detected. ..."
        export NVIDIA_CPU_ONLY=1
      fi

  A working GPU, and the image tells every framework in it to use the CPU.

--- (B) alone: guest ldconfig hook, SONAME mounts stripped -----------------
$ python3 -c 'strip every mount whose "why" starts with SONAME'
  mounts 76 -> 53 (SONAME entries removed)
$ docker run --runtime=nvkvm-kata --gpus all --rm <img> bash -c '...'
  NVIDIA_CPU_ONLY=unset
  lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 20 Aug 28 02:55 /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1
        -> libcuda.so.575.51.03
  libcuda.so.1 (libc6,x86-64) => /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1
  GPU 0: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070 Ti SUPER

  So (B) alone WORKS TOO, and it is the more complete of the two: ldconfig
  creates the SONAME symlink ITSELF from the versioned file.  This confirms
  that NVIDIA's `create-symlinks` hook is NOT what makes libcuda.so.1 exist --
  `update-ldcache` is.  (nvidia-ctk's create-symlinks emits only the odd ones
  out: libcuda.so, libGLX_indirect.so.0, the gbm and xorg links.  See
  nvidia-ctk-cdi-generate.json.)

--- (A) + (B): what ships -------------------------------------------------
  NVIDIA_CPU_ONLY=unset
  ldconfig -p | grep libcuda.so.1
      libcuda.so.1 (libc6,x86-64) => /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1
  ldd /usr/bin/nvidia-smi | grep -c "not found"      -> 0
  mount | grep -c nvidia                             -> 66

  ldconfig indexes the SONAME-mounted files happily (their DT_SONAME already
  matches their filename, so it has no link to create and nothing to rename
  over a mount point).  Keeping both costs 23 mount entries and removes a
  silent-failure mode: an image with no ldconfig (distroless) cannot run (B),
  and would otherwise get a GPU with no libcuda.so.1 and no error.
